<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luis Carrero Blanco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luis Carrero Blanco was a Spanish admiral and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a>'s closest political adviser for three decades, serving as under-secretary and then minister of the presidency. In June 1973 Franco made him prime minister, the first since the civil war, expecting him to ensure the regime's continuity. On 20 December 1973 the Basque separatist group ETA killed him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> with a bomb that hurled his car over a building.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luis-carrero-blanco.jpg" alt="Luis Carrero Blanco" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vélez, José, Periodista gráfico) (, 1933-2012 Sierra, F., Fotógrafo Diario Región (Oviedo) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luis_Carrero_Blanco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Luis Carrero Blanco was born on 4 March 1904 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santona">Santoña</a>, in the province of Santander, the son of an army officer, Camilo Carrero Gutiérrez, and Ángeles Blanco Abascal. He entered the Spanish Naval Academy at San Fernando, Cádiz, in January 1918 and was commissioned in September 1922. After the Submarine School course of 1924 he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rif-war">Rif War</a>, including the landings at Alcazarseguer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hoceima">Alhucemas</a> in 1925. In 1929 he married María del Carmen Pichot y Villa and was given command of the submarine B-5.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> broke out Carrero Blanco was teaching submarine tactics at the Naval Warfare College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. He took refuge first in the Mexican embassy and then in the French one, crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and in July 1937 entered the Nationalist zone through San Sebastián. He served as naval liaison to the Army of the North, commanded the destroyer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> and the submarine General Sanjurjo, and in August 1939 became Chief of Operations of the Navy General Staff.</p><p>In May 1941 Franco appointed him, at 37, Under-Secretary of the Presidency of the Government. After the Begoña incident of 1942 he advised Franco to remove Ramón Serrano Suñer from his ministry and from the Political Junta of the Falange, which Franco did. The historian Joan Maria Thomàs calls him extremely faithful and submissive to the Caudillo. He became a vice-admiral in 1963 and an admiral in 1966, was the main drafter of the 1947 Law of Succession, and was made Deputy Prime Minister on 21 September 1967. When Juan Carlos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> was named Franco's heir in 1969, many expected an authoritarian monarchy guided by Carrero to carry Francoism on without Franco.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Franco appointed him Prime Minister on 8 June 1973. Six months later, on 20 December 1973, four members of an ETA cell killed him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> with a bomb placed near the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> de Borja on Calle de Serrano, as he returned from daily mass. His funeral at the Basilica of Saint Francis the Great was one of Franco's last public appearances, and he was buried at Mingorrubio Cemetery in El Pardo.</p><p>The assassination, followed by the overthrow of the Portuguese regime in 1974, dealt a blow to those who favoured a continuation of the regime. His successor, Carlos Arias Navarro, promised in February 1974 reforms including the right to form political associations. Historians such as Antonio Elorza describe Carrero's ideology as counter-revolutionary, anticommunist and hostile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freemasonry">Freemasonry</a>, and he is considered a pure Francoist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luis Carrero Blanco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1973 Franco resigned as Prime Minister and named Admiral <b>Luis Carrero Blanco</b>, who was meant to continue the regime, but ETA assassinated him the same year and Carlos Arias Navarro took his place.</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Santoña, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Fourth professed house in Madrid, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.44,-3.46&amp;year=1948" aria-label="Santoña on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4467 623 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Luis Carrero Blanco"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4917 893h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4917 893h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.44,-3.46&amp;year=1948">What was here in 1948? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
